    "A handful of men sit up late in a house down at the Piraeus, Athens' port, on the night of a brand-new festival, and one of them asks a question that sounds like dinner-party small talk and turns out to have no floor under it: what is justice, really, and is the just person actually better off? Plato's answer is a single book, the *Republic*, in Greek the *Politeia*, written around 375 BCE, and it is the most ambitious thing anyone in the West had attempted: a single connected argument that runs from a squabble about paying your debts, through the founding of an entire imaginary city, up to the highest object the human mind can reach, and back down again to the afterlife. Everything famous in it (the cave, the philosopher-king, the noble lie, the ring that makes you invisible) is a move inside that one argument, not a detachable highlight.",
