          "p": "The *Republic* opens with a man walking home from church. \"I went down yesterday to the Piraeus with Glaucon the son of Ariston,\" Socrates begins, going down to Athens' port to pray to a goddess at a new festival (the worship of Bendis, a Thracian deity Jowett glosses as \"the Thracian Artemis\"), and to see how the newcomers would run the celebration. He gets waylaid on the way back, dragged to the house of a wealthy arms-manufacturer named Cephalus, and there, almost by accident, falls into the conversation that becomes the longest single argument Plato ever wrote. The setting is ordinary: not a courtroom, not a lecture hall, just a house full of people after a party, where someone says something loose about justice and a philosopher cannot let it go."
