          "p": "Then comes the most notorious single device in the city's founding: the **royal lie**. Socrates proposes that the rulers tell all the citizens a deliberate falsehood: \"one royal lie which may deceive the rulers, if that be possible, and at any rate the rest of the city.\" (You have almost certainly heard this as the \"noble lie,\" which is the standard textbook name, but Jowett's translation of the dialogue itself says *royal lie*. The Greek phrase, *gennaion pseudos*, has \"noble\" in the sense of *well-born, of good stock*, a lie bred into the city's foundations, rather than \"noble\" in the modern sense of morally fine, which is why the popular label quietly misleads.) The lie has two parts. First, that all the citizens were born from the earth itself, brothers and sisters of one soil, so their first loyalty is to the city and to each other. Second, the **myth of the metals**: that the god who made them mixed *gold* into the souls of the rulers, *silver* into the soldiers, and *brass and iron* into the farmers and craftsmen, and that each person's metal fixes their proper place."
