          "p": "The myth is constantly misremembered as a rigid caste system, and the text builds in the opposite. The lie *explicitly* allows mobility: \"a golden parent will sometimes have a silver son, or a silver parent a golden son,\" and when that happens the child must be reassigned to the class their metal fits, demoted or promoted regardless of birth. So it is not a hereditary aristocracy of blood; it is a sorting by aptitude, dressed up as a divine origin story to make people accept it. That does not make it innocent. Plato really does have his rulers tell the whole population a falsehood to secure the social order, with their eyes open, and a modern reader is right to find that disturbing. But it is not the fixed-caste, master-race fiction it is often turned into. The text says: a founding lie, told for cohesion, that nonetheless insists a brass-souled child of golden parents must take the lower place."
